the 2015 Ford Mustang GT convertible is  very proud of itself it has every reason  to be the 50th anniversary model of the  iconic pony car has a 5.0 liter v8  engine that gets 435 horsepower and hits  60 miles per hour in 4.7 seconds it has  a noble muscular profile with that  unmistakable long and strong nose the  18-inch wheels look hot too like they  can take on any old Dodge or Chevy or  revving enticingly in the other way plus  it's a convertible what's not to like  well a few things let's start with the  interior a space defined by the most  uncomfortable car seats I've ever  experienced the headrests hit the middle  of my neck and there was no lumbar  support to be found the seats feel  chintzy and the covering on the steering  wheels feels cheap the shifter on the  six-speed automatic transmission feels  flimsy to use as well and it all too  easily swoops past years it's not worth  the 1,200 dollars extra you'll have to  pay for it I also take issue with the  diminutive size of the Mustang side  mirrors they're way too small  the rear visibility in here could do  with some major improvement of course  the visibility improves exponentially  the moment you take down this black  ragtop this car is meant to be driven  and shown as a convertible once the roof  is down the automatic headlamps fog  lamps dual exhausts and sporty spoiler  really pop  punch the gas and the Mustang g/t will  lunge forward with the passion of a  buckin bronc it's a great feeling I just  wish the price tag felt as good the  $42,000 base MSRP seems a little steep  for what is certainly not as  well-appointed as other vehicles in this  price range when the Mustang first came  out in 1964 it was financially  accessible even to people on a  Secretary's budget which directly  influence its rabid popularity that was  kind of the whole idea this one while  admittedly sexy on the outside and more  than capable on the road doesn't have  quite the same spirit
 

